@* One title -- a film or a series -- as a poster and visible text. Rendered inside PosterGrid's list item;
   it is not itself the <li>, so this component stays renderable on its own and the list semantics stay
   with the grid.

   IT KNOWS NOTHING ABOUT FILMS, and that is deliberate. Everything it draws arrives as a TileView, whose
   remarks record why one component serves both walls rather than a second component with a second copy of
   PosterTile.razor.css. The subject-specific judgement -- which facts to show, and which to leave out
   rather than show as zero -- lives in the mapping, not here.

   ONE ANCHOR, AND ITS BOUNDARY IS THE DESIGN. .tile-body -- the poster and the text block, and nothing
   else -- is the link. The confidence badge and ActionSlot are SIBLINGS of it, and each for its own
   reason: a <button> inside an <a> is invalid and unusable, so the favourite control that goes in
   ActionSlot could never be a descendant; and the badge's words would be swallowed into the link's
   accessible name, so "Se7en 1995 1h 50m 8.4/10" would become "Se7en 1995 1h 50m 8.4/10 Needs review" on
   every degraded tile. Part A built both slots in this position in advance so that this task moved no
   markup, and PosterTileTests reads the boundary from the other side.

   WHAT THE ANCHOR SETTLED, recorded because Part A recorded its absence. The plan's requirement table
   asked for "a real accessible name on every tile" and "a keyboard-operable grid". With no anchor the tile
   was not a named widget at all and its only boundary was the grid's list item; with one, every tile is a
   named link and the whole wall is reachable by keyboard rather than just the pager. *@

<div class="tile">
    @* ONE ANCHOR AROUND .tile-body AND NOTHING ELSE, exactly where the comment above reserved it. Its
       accessible name is everything inside it, in DOM order: "Se7en 1995 1h 50m 8.4/10" -- the poster is
       alt="" and contributes nothing, which is why it can be decorative here. That name is read on each of
       60 tiles, and it is the reason the facts in the caption stay terse.

       NO aria-label AND NO title. The visible text IS the name; a label would let the two diverge and both
       attributes are banned on this tile by StandingAuditTests and PosterTileTests. No aria-current either:
       a tile is not a navigation item.

       href comes from the mapping and is RELATIVE there, for the reason TileView records. Blazor turns the
       activation into client-side routing; GitHub Pages serves 404.html -- a copy of index.html -- for a
       cold deep link, so the address survives a reload too. *@
    <a class="tile-body" href="@Tile.Href">
        @* alt="" because the title is visible text in this same tile: a description here would be the
           title announced twice. Never an img whose src is the poster path with an empty-string fallback --
           an empty src can render broken-image chrome and some browsers expose the URL as text, so the
           no-poster case is a CSS box with no img in it at all.

           width and height are the intrinsic size of a w342 poster. They reserve the box before the TMDB
           CDN answers, which matters most under magnification, and EveryImgHasExplicitWidthAndHeight
           requires them rather than preferring them. *@
        @if (Tile.Poster is { Length: > 0 } poster)
        {
            <img src="@($"https://image.tmdb.org/t/p/w342{poster}")" alt=""
                 width="342" height="513" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        }
        else
        {
            <span class="poster-placeholder" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        }

        @* Truncation is CSS only, and there is none of it: the full title is in the DOM and on the screen.
           Cutting the string in C# would destroy the accessible name permanently. *@
        <span class="tile-title">@Tile.Title</span>

        @* A year, so never formatted with N0: 1,995 would be wrong. Only the grid head's total is formatted.
           A series shows the year it STARTED here, which is the same kind of fact in the same position. *@
        @if (Tile.Year is { } year)
        {
            <span class="tile-year">@year</span>
        }

        @* THE CAPTION FACTS, all INSIDE .tile-body and never siblings of it. PosterTileTests pins
           ".tile > *" at one child for a confident record, and this is caption text, so the caption is
           where it belongs. Each fact carries its own class so the caption can be read back by name;
           none of them is styled individually -- .tile-meta > span dresses them all.

           NO SEPARATOR CHARACTER IN THE DOM. The gap between facts is a flex gap; a middot is read
           inconsistently, and moving it into ::before does not hide it from the accessibility tree.

           AN EMPTY CAPTION renders an empty .tile-meta, and that is correct rather than untidy: the
           element has no border, no background and no min-height, so it occupies nothing, and a guard
           here would be a second rule about absence sitting on top of the mapping's. *@
        <span class="tile-meta">
            @foreach (var fact in Tile.Meta)
            {
                <span class="@fact.Class">@fact.Text</span>
            }
        </span>
    </a>

    @* THE CONFIDENCE BADGE, in the slot the comment above reserved for it: a sibling of .tile-body, so it
       is outside the anchor. That position is the requirement, not a preference -- a badge inside the
       anchor would be swallowed into the link's name.

       Plain visible text and nothing else. No label and no attribute of any kind carries it, so what a
       reader meets after the title and the year is the same words the screen shows: "An Unlisted Film",
       then "Not matched". Nothing announces it either -- a record's own quality is a property of the
       record, not a change to report.

       THE WORDS AND THE RULE BOTH COME FROM ConfidenceBand AND ARE NEVER RESTATED HERE.
       MovieLibrary.Enrich's job summary counts the same bands through the same class, so the badge on
       screen and the build summary the owner reads have to say the same thing; a second copy of either the
       strings or the I == 0 precedence is exactly how the two drift apart. BadgeFor returns null for
       Confident, which is why the ordinary case renders nothing at all rather than a third variant: a
       badge on 1,265 of 1,456 records would train the eye to ignore the ones that matter. *@
    @if (ConfidenceBand.BadgeFor(Tile.Band) is { } badge)
    {
        <span class="badge @VariantClass(Tile.Band)">@badge</span>
    }

    @ActionSlot
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// What to draw. See <see cref="TileView"/> for why the subject-specific decisions are not here.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public required TileView Tile { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Part B's favourite or watched control. Empty in Part A, and kept as a parameter so that the slot it
    /// occupies -- a sibling of <c>.tile-body</c> -- is fixed now rather than negotiated later.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? ActionSlot { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The variant class for a band that carries a badge: the band's own name in lower case, so
    /// <c>badge-unmatched</c> and <c>badge-uncertain</c>.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Derived from the enum rather than written out twice, and checked in BOTH directions by
    /// <c>DegradedRecordTests.EveryBadgeVariantTheTileRendersHasARuleAndEveryRuleHasAVariant</c>: a band
    /// renamed in Core cannot leave a styled class nothing renders, and a renamed rule in
    /// <c>PosterTile.razor.css</c> cannot leave a rendered class nothing styles. Either way round the
    /// badge would still appear -- the shared <c>.badge</c> rule would draw it -- in the wrong colour and
    /// with nothing on screen to say so, which is why neither direction is left to a reader to notice.
    /// <c>Confidence.Confident</c> never reaches here, because <c>ConfidenceBand.BadgeFor</c> returns null
    /// for it and the badge is not rendered at all.
    /// </remarks>
    private static string VariantClass(Confidence band) => "badge-" + band.ToString().ToLowerInvariant();
}
